<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Bora Bora</h1><p class="st-meta">1700 CE – 1888 CE (approx.) · capital Vaitape</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A warrior kingdom of the Society Islands whose chiefs conquered neighbouring Raiatea and Tahaʻa and remained independent until French annexation in 1888.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bora-bora">Bora Bora</a>, a high island ringed by a wide lagoon in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leeward-islands-society-islands">Leeward Society Islands</a>, was known in tradition as Porapora. Its chiefs were famed warriors, and in the mid-18th century the chief Puni led conquests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahaa">Tahaʻa</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-raiatea">Raiatea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maupiti">Maupiti</a>, making Bora Bora the leading power of the Leeward group when Europeans first arrived in the 1760s.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kings ruled through a hierarchy of chiefs, with the marae of Fare Opu and others serving as ritual centres. The island's warriors and canoes gave it influence over its neighbours, and marriage ties linked its royal family to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-raiatea">Raiatea</a> and to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-tahiti">Kingdom of Tahiti</a>. Missionaries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london-missionary-society">London Missionary Society</a> arrived in the 1820s, and the island adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and written laws.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarnac-convention">Jarnac Convention</a> of 1847 recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bora-bora">Bora Bora's</a> independence. Its last queen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teriimaevarua-iii">Teriimaevarua III</a>, reigned from 1873 until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> annexed the island in 1888. It became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-polynesia">French Polynesia</a> and, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> supply base.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-bora-bora.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Bora Bora" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Ivan Sache · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Kingdom_of_Bora_Bora_(1837-1842).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1700 CE – 1888 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vaitape</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Puni, Teriimaevarua III</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Polynesian religion, later Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tahitian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Porapora</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1794&amp;focus=kingdom-of-bora-bora" aria-label="Kingdom of Bora Bora on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="548 2791 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Bora Bora lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M868 3047L938 3085"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M938 3085h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M868 3047h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M868 3047h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Vaitape. <a href="/atlas?year=1794&amp;focus=kingdom-of-bora-bora">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
